{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.List.Perm.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.List.Lemmas","lean:module:Mathlib.Order.Defs.PartialOrder","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Nat.Cast.Defs"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity.letterSumCost_fails_to_separate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity.letterSumCost_fails_to_separate","kind":"theorem","line":256,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/CostOrderSensitivity.lean#L256-L260","doc":"A letter-sum cost really does fail to separate a concrete distinct pair:\nthose two words have equal cost under **every** weight. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity.exists_distinct_perm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity.exists_distinct_perm","kind":"theorem","line":252,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/CostOrderSensitivity.lean#L252-L254","doc":"Two **distinct** words that are permutations of one another. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity.letterSumCost_not_determined","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity.letterSumCost_not_determined","kind":"theorem","line":231,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/CostOrderSensitivity.lean#L231-L243","doc":"The same for an arbitrary letter-sum cost — crossing count, writhe, and\n`E_cross` as measured are all instances. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity.lengthCost_not_determined","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity.lengthCost_not_determined","kind":"theorem","line":214,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/CostOrderSensitivity.lean#L214-L229","doc":"**The corpus's crossing-count cost does not determine the word.** Two\ndistinct feasible candidates that rearrange one another are both selected.\n\n`π` is the length; `f` is the cast. Because `List.length` is `Perm`-invariant,\nthe fibre hypothesis is supplied by the rearrangement itself. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity.length_eq_letterSumCost","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity.length_eq_letterSumCost","kind":"theorem","line":204,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/CostOrderSensitivity.lean#L204-L212","doc":"The corpus's own cost — `fun w => (w.length : R)`, the crossing count — is\nthe letter-sum cost with unit weight, hence is covered. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity.letterSumCost_perm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity.letterSumCost_perm","kind":"theorem","line":194,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/CostOrderSensitivity.lean#L194-L202","doc":"**The invariance, additive form.** A letter-sum cost is blind to word order.\n\nThis is the additive twin of the already-held\n`QOU.QBeta.OptionELevelAcceptanceTest.monoidHom_order_blind`, and is stated here\nonly so the instantiations below can use it directly; the *result* is not new. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity.letterSumCost_cons","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity.letterSumCost_cons","kind":"theorem","line":190,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/CostOrderSensitivity.lean#L190-L192","doc":"A letter-sum cost splits off its head letter. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity.letterSumCost","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity.letterSumCost","kind":"def","line":184,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/CostOrderSensitivity.lean#L184-L188","doc":"A **letter-sum cost**: the cost of a word is the sum of a per-letter weight.\nCrossing count is `weight ≡ 1`; writhe is `weight = ±1`; the crossing energy\n`E_cross` measured in `qou-99qc` is `weight = ±h`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity.selected_not_determined_of_cost_factors","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity.selected_not_determined_of_cost_factors","kind":"theorem","line":160,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/CostOrderSensitivity.lean#L160-L178","doc":"**The step `FrobeniusSelected.cost_eq` stops short of.** If two *distinct*\nfeasible candidates lie in the same fibre of a quotient the cost factors\nthrough, then the minimal-cost selection predicate holds of **both**, so it does\nnot determine the word.\n\nThe three conjuncts are exactly `FrobeniusSelected`'s `mem`, `feasible` and\n`minimal`, spelled out rather than by re-declaring that structure — this is a\nstatement *about* the corpus's selection rule, not a second copy of it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity.minimal_transfers_along_fibre","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.CostOrderSensitivity.minimal_transfers_along_fibre","kind":"theorem","line":147,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/CostOrderSensitivity.lean#L147-L158","doc":"**Minimality transfers along the fibre.** If the cost is `f ∘ π` and `w₂`\nlies in the same fibre of `π` as a cost-minimal `w₁`, then `w₂` is cost-minimal\ntoo. Nothing about words is used: this is a statement about minimising a\nfunction that is constant on fibres. ","signatureChunk":0}]}